n is one of the big arguments  against banning the burqa now many hate  the burqa because it represents  controlled by men over women in Islam  counter to that though Pat is that if  you tell women you can't wear this  you're also controlling women's bodies  aren't you yeah I guess it's a catch-22  in that sense I mean I'm pretty split on  this just coming from a more American  perspective of having freedom or village  and having that long tradition but you  know it goes both ways I'm on the fence  about it this is something a lot of our  viewers are actually on the fence about  and I there's no doubt that there's no  way someone should be able to enter a  bank with their face covered it just  makes absolutely no sense you go into a  bank people have to know who you are any  situation where you have to show ID you  can't go in with your face covered you  just can't you need to be able to  confirm that the person on the ID is the  person that's there the argument in  public out on the street is much more  difficult to make but you could pass a  law that says in particular situations  all face coverings must be lifted it  doesn't matter whether it's a mask or a  helmet or a religious item this includes  airport security this includes banks  this might include retail stores  wherever I just don't know that that's a  solution because the biggest debate here  is over oppressive ideology the idea  that well sometimes women make the  choice they want the burqa but are they  making that choice in a way that is  already based on an oppressive ideology  that has been ingrained into them it  ignores though that women do have a  right to dress however they want if  that's how they truly want to but we've  many shows have talked about this just  because a woman says they want the burqa  is it disrespectful to women to say you  don't really want it you only think you  want it because men told you you want it  either way you can make a pretty  compelling case yeah that's the thing